#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125392} :activity/id #uuid "7fb60c61-c60a-5530-b6e6-eb3f36962ebb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125781 :tutorial/content "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{S}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}^{S}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}}y \\\\ {\\color{blue}{c}}x+{\\color{blue}{d}}y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{blue}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "439421f9-bf09-5277-910e-bc279b222542" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3527 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47152a53-794a-571b-bae6-bbcab9ffc719" :step/math-academy-id 20001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124512 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124321} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e73b8bcf-79c2-5326-b396-d7d3e06badaa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604076}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "847da92e-200b-512e-810c-f5ebd91868d8" :step/math-academy-id 20002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124513 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124322} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2776554-94ce-5c25-b838-16fdfceddb1c" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125232} {:db/id #edb/ref 1